<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    Dark palette, in two layers.

    LAYER ONE is the SCALE: twelve neutral steps, each with one job. A view never names a step.
    LAYER TWO is the ROLE tokens below it: every brush a view can name points at a step.

    Why two layers. A palette of six flat names cannot answer "may this colour go here" - it only says
    what the colour is. A scale answers it, because each step carries an assignment, and a contrast
    failure is then almost always a WRONG STEP rather than a wrong value. Measured on this palette
    before the rebuild: the single stroke colour was doing three different jobs at once (divider,
    control border, focus-adjacent edge) and failed the border one at 1.34 against a card.

    One theme only, but the split means a second theme would be a new set of step values, not a rewrite
    of views (zasady/13 section 7). Meaning never rides on colour alone: a shape or label always
    accompanies a status colour (section 9).
  -->

  <!--
    ============================ LAYER ONE: the neutral scale ============================

    Steps are declared as Color, not Brush, so a role token can point at one by name. Views must not
    name a step directly - they name a role below.

    The axis is the one this product already had: R equals G, B a touch higher, which reads slightly
    cool rather than dead grey. Steps 1 to 3 and 11 to 12 keep the exact values the shipped build uses,
    so adopting the scale changes nothing that is already on screen. Steps 4 to 8 are new and fill the
    gaps the contrast measurement exposed.

    Every threshold below is MEASURED, not asserted. Numbers are WCAG relative-luminance ratios.
  -->

  <!-- 1-2: application background, and the other surface (card, chrome strip). 1.14 apart - the same
       distance GitHub dark (1.09) and VS Code dark (1.08) use between editor and sidebar. A card needs
       no border to read as a card at this distance, which is why cards here carry none. -->
  <Color x:Key="Neutral01">#FF1B1B1F</Color>
  <Color x:Key="Neutral02">#FF26262B</Color>

  <!-- 3-5: control fill, by state. The mouse answers on FILL, the keyboard answers on EDGE (step 7),
       so a control that is hovered AND focused still says both things (guide section 10.9).

       🔴 Pressed is DARKER than rest, not lighter, and that is deliberate. The first draft had it
       lighter and the arithmetic closed against itself: a fill bright enough to read as "pressed"
       pushed secondary text on it down to 4.12 against a floor of 4.5. Pressed as a RECESS - the
       WinUI behaviour - solves it without touching the ink: secondary text lands at 7.61 instead.
       Do not "fix" this back to a lighter pressed fill without redoing that measurement. -->
  <Color x:Key="Neutral03">#FF2F2F35</Color>
  <Color x:Key="Neutral04">#FF3E3E46</Color>
  <Color x:Key="Neutral05">#FF232328</Color>

  <!-- 6-8: lines and edges, three separate jobs that used to share one value.
       6 divider   - a quiet rule between sections. No threshold: it separates, it does not identify.
       7 border    - the edge that says "this is a control". 3.90 on the window, 3.42 on a card,
                     3.02 on its own rest fill. WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3.0 against ADJACENT background, so
                     those three are the pairs that matter. The ratio against the control's own HOVER
                     fill is 2.41 and is deliberately not a gate: that edge is interior to one control,
                     not the boundary that identifies it, and a hovered control is more visible, not
                     less. Widening the gate to that pair reddens on nothing a user can see.
       8 muted glyph - chevrons, separators, a highlighted edge. 6.13 on the window, 5.38 on a card. -->
  <Color x:Key="Neutral06">#FF3A3A42</Color>
  <Color x:Key="Neutral07">#FF777787</Color>
  <Color x:Key="Neutral08">#FF9999AB</Color>

  <!-- 9-10 are the accent fill and its hover, and they are not neutral - see the accent block below. -->

  <!-- 11-12: ink. Secondary holds 5.15 or better on every fill it is painted on, primary 9.48 or
       better. Both were already right and are carried over unchanged. -->
  <Color x:Key="Neutral11">#FFB4B4BE</Color>
  <Color x:Key="Neutral12">#FFF2F2F4</Color>

  <!--
    Accent. One accent, on the primary action and on the focus ring, and nowhere else - an accent on
    three elements reads as a decision, on twelve as decoration.

    🔴 This accent carries a DARK label, never a white one. Measured: dark ink on it is 8.56, white ink
    is 2.01 against a floor of 4.5. That is a property of the hue, not a preference, and it closes the
    white-on-accent option for the whole application. A later session that "fixes" a dark button label
    to white will drop it under the floor with no visible symptom.
  -->
  <Color x:Key="AccentBase">#FF4CC2FF</Color>
  <Color x:Key="AccentRing">#FF7CD0FF</Color>

  <!--
    ============================ LAYER TWO: role tokens ============================

    These are the only names a view may use. Each points at a step above.
  -->

  <!-- Surfaces. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushBgBase" Color="{StaticResource Neutral01}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushBgSurface" Color="{StaticResource Neutral02}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushBgSurfaceAlt" Color="{StaticResource Neutral03}" />

  <!-- Control fill by state. New roles: nothing names them until the parts library lands. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushControlRest" Color="{StaticResource Neutral03}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushControlHover" Color="{StaticResource Neutral04}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushControlPressed" Color="{StaticResource Neutral05}" />

  <!-- Lines and edges. BrushStroke is the OLD single name and still points at the divider step, so
       today's views keep the exact pixels they have. It goes when the last view stops naming it. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushStroke" Color="{StaticResource Neutral06}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushDivider" Color="{StaticResource Neutral06}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushControlBorder" Color="{StaticResource Neutral07}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushGlyphMuted" Color="{StaticResource Neutral08}" />

  <!-- Ink. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushTextPrimary" Color="{StaticResource Neutral12}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushTextSecondary" Color="{StaticResource Neutral11}" />

  <!-- Accent and keyboard focus (focus ring must be visibly high-contrast, section 9). -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushAccent" Color="{StaticResource AccentBase}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushFocusRing" Color="{StaticResource AccentRing}" />

  <!-- The label that sits ON the accent fill. Named so the pairing is one decision in one place,
       rather than a colour chosen again at every primary button. See the accent note above. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushOnAccent" Color="{StaticResource Neutral01}" />

  <!-- Verdict status colours. Always paired with a shape and a label, never colour alone. Each holds
       5.28 or better against both the window and a card. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushStatusWorks" Color="#FF4EC9A8" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushStatusPartial" Color="#FFE5C07B" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushStatusFails" Color="#FFF0736A" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrushStatusUndetermined" Color="#FF9AA0A6" />

</ResourceDictionary>
